# Astrolabe Books — Cursor Rules
# SDD+ Methodology v1.0

## RULE 0 — READ BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE
Before any action in this repository:
1. Read sdd_strategy/README.md
2. Read sdd_strategy/specs/global_spec.md
3. Read sdd_strategy/specs/global_tech_spec.md
4. Read the relevant domain spec files for this task
5. Apply the Freshness Protocol

## RULE 1 — FRESHNESS PROTOCOL (MANDATORY BEFORE EVERY TASK)
Check Last reviewed date on all related spec files.
0 stale: proceed immediately.
1-3 stale (> 7 days): update them before proceeding.
4+ stale: create a task in global_task_spec.md and stop. Notify the user.

## RULE 2 — SPEC BEFORE CODE
Never write implementation code for something not in a spec.
If a task requires something not in the specs, update the spec first, then implement.

## RULE 3 — ARCHITECTURE DEPENDENCY RULES
Domain         -> no external project references, zero external NuGet packages
Application    -> Domain only
Infrastructure -> Application + Domain
Presentation   -> Application + Infrastructure
Tests          -> only the project under test
Never add a reference that violates these rules.

## RULE 4 — CQRS RULES
Commands implement ICommand, handlers return Task<Result>
Queries implement IQuery<T>, handlers return Task<Result<T>>
Inject ISender in controllers and jobs (not IMediator unless Publish() is needed)
Validate inside handlers — no pipeline behavior classes
Use built-in logging tools — do not create a LoggingBehavior class
CancellationToken is always the last parameter and is propagated to every async call

## RULE 5 — TASK TRACKING (MANDATORY AFTER EVERY TASK)
Mark the task with a completed marker in the relevant tasks.md
Add a row to the Completion Log with date and notes
Update the progress percentage in the Progress Summary
Update global_task_spec.md if it was a global or cross-domain task

## RULE 6 — NO ASSUMPTIONS
If something is ambiguous or not covered by the specs:
- Do NOT assume and proceed
- Ask the user for clarification
- Reference the specific spec section that is unclear
Wait for clarification before writing any code.

## RULE 7 — DOMAIN GROWTH MONITORING
If you detect any domain exceeding these thresholds:
  - more than 20 business rules
  - more than 40 tasks
  - more than 15 commands + queries combined
  - more than 8 entities / aggregates
  - more than 600 lines in technical.md
Create a task in global_task_spec.md immediately.
Do NOT split the domain without explicit human approval.
Domains currently under watch: catalog, billing, identity.

## RULE 8 — SECURITY (NON-NEGOTIABLE)
Secrets, API keys, passwords, connection strings: never in committed files.
Use environment variables or a secrets manager for development.
Passwords: always hash before persistence. Never store or log plain text.
API error responses: never include stack traces or internal exception details.
AI provider keys are encrypted at rest and never returned by any API response.

## RULE 9 — COMMIT FORMAT
{type}({task-id}): {description}
Types: feat | fix | test | refactor | docs | chore
Example: feat(IDN-001): add RegisterCommand handler
One task per commit. Pass all existing tests before committing.

## PROJECT-SPECIFIC RULES

## RULE 10 — AUTHORITY PRECEDENCE
1. The prototype in docs/design/ — product behaviour, UI, copy, business rules
2. SDD_PLIUS_STRATEGY.md — process, structure, spec format, agent rules
3. GUIDELINES.md — architecture and stack where the two above are silent
Never invent product behaviour. Read docs/design/prototype.source.js.

## RULE 11 — UI RECONSTRUCTION
The prototype uses inline styles, not Material UI.
It is a visual and behavioural reference, never reusable code.
Rebuild every screen on the Material UI theme defined in GUIDELINES.md section 38.1.
Do not copy inline styles into the codebase.

## RULE 12 — MONEY
Every monetary amount is an integer number of cents.
Never decimal, float, or double for money, in any layer, including DTOs and the database.

## RULE 13 — COVERAGE MINIMUMS
Domain 90% · Application 80% · Infrastructure 70% · Presentation 70% · Frontend 80%
Every BR-* business rule has at least one unit test.
No real HTTP calls or real database connections in unit tests.
Each EF InMemory test gets a unique database name.

## RULE 15 — FILE AND FOLDER ORGANISATION (NON-NEGOTIABLE)
One public type per file. The file name matches the type name.
Never group several interfaces or entities into one file.

Each layer segregates by kind, and the namespace matches the folder:

  Domain/
      Abstractions/              base types shared by every domain
          Repositories/          IRepository<TEntity>
      Primitives/                Result, Error, Money
      {Domain}/
          Entities/
          ValueObjects/
          Events/
          Errors/
          Repositories/          the domain's repository interfaces

  Application/
      Abstractions/              interfaces only
      Contracts/                 DTOs, messages, results
      {Domain}/Commands|Queries/

  Infrastructure/
      Persistence/
          Configurations/        one EF configuration per entity
          Repositories/          one implementation per entity
          Seeding/
      {Concern}/                 Email, Time, ...

  Presentation/
      Controllers/ Middleware/ Options/ Extensions/

## RULE 16 — REPOSITORY PATTERN
Generic operations live on IRepository<TEntity> in Domain/Abstractions/Repositories.
Every concrete contract extends it and adds only domain-specific capabilities:

    public interface IReservationRepository : IRepository<Reservation>

Implementations extend the base Repository<TEntity> in Infrastructure.
Never declare a generic method again on a concrete interface.
IQueryable, DbContext and EF tracking concepts never leave Infrastructure.

## RULE 17 — FEATURE FOLDERS (NON-NEGOTIABLE)
Application and Infrastructure group every domain under Features/.
A new domain gets a folder there and nowhere else.

  Application/
      Abstractions/          interfaces only, grouped by concern
      Contracts/             DTOs, messages, results
      Features/
          {Domain}/
              Commands/{Name}/    one folder per command: the command and its handler
              Queries/{Name}/     one folder per query
              Services/           application services shared inside the domain

A feature folder contains only Commands, Queries and Services. A cross-cutting concern such as mail
composition is NOT a feature: it lives in its own top-level folder (Application/Mail/), with one
template class per domain and a single shared options type. Putting it inside a feature adds a
fourth kind of folder and duplicates shared configuration once per domain.

  Infrastructure/
      Features/{Domain}/     domain-specific implementations
      Persistence/
          Configurations/{Domain}/
          Repositories/{Domain}/
          Converters/ Migrations/ Seeding/
      Integrations/{Provider}/    external providers: Mail, Ai, Payments
      Time/

Never place a domain folder directly under Application/ or Infrastructure/.

## RULE 18 — UNIT OF WORK
IUnitOfWork lives in Domain/Abstractions/Persistence and exposes SaveChangesAsync plus
ExecuteInTransactionAsync.

Every bounded context has its own unit of work extending it, exposing ONLY that context's
repositories:

    public interface IIdentityUnitOfWork : IUnitOfWork
    {
        IUserRepository Users { get; }
        IUserSessionRepository Sessions { get; }
    }

Handlers depend on the unit of work, never on repositories directly. That keeps constructors small
while a handler in one context still cannot see another context's repositories.

Every repository reachable from a unit of work shares one change tracker, so a single
SaveChangesAsync commits their staged work atomically. Repositories never call SaveChanges.

Do not inject IDbContextFactory into a request-scoped handler: each call returns a new context with
its own change tracker, which silently breaks the unit of work. The factory is only for work outside
a request scope, such as a background job.

## RULE 19 — DOMAIN EVENTS
Aggregates raise events; SaveChangesAsync dispatches them after the commit succeeds.

Anything that must happen whenever a state change occurs — regardless of which handler caused it —
belongs in an event handler, not in each caller. Cache eviction on session revocation is the
reference example: four rules end a session, and every one of them must evict, so the eviction is
driven by the event and cannot be forgotten.

A reaction runs after the commit, so it can be retried or lost. Never put a step the business
outcome depends on in an event handler.

## RULE 20 — NO CATCH-ALL FOLDERS
A feature contains exactly three kinds: Commands, Queries and Events — the three message kinds of
CQRS. There is no Services, Helpers, Utils or Common folder inside a feature.

Logic shared across features is a concern with its own top-level folder under Shared/, named for
what it is. If shared logic reacts to a state change, it is an event handler instead.

## RULE 21 — COMMIT AT THE END OF EVERY STAGE
A stage is not finished until its work is committed.

Before committing, ALWAYS ask the user for approval. Never commit unprompted. Present first:
  - the full build result (0 errors, 0 warnings)
  - the full test result
  - the file count and anything unusual in the change
  - proof that no secret is staged

Never stage .env or any file holding a real key, password or connection string. Verify it, do not
assume .gitignore covered it.

Commit message format is RULE 9: {type}({task-id}): {description}. A stage-closing commit uses the
plan and stage as its identifier, for example: feat(PLAN-001-stage-1): identity and network.

Pushing is a separate action and needs its own approval.

## RULE 22 — VERIFY AGAINST THE RUNNING SYSTEM, NOT ONLY THE TESTS
A green test suite is not evidence that a feature works.

Before marking a task done, exercise it against the running composition: sign in, call the endpoint,
read the response. Stage 2 shipped two defects that every unit test passed — a value converter that
made catalogue search fail at run time, and a seeder projection that crashed the API at startup.
Both were found by curl, neither by dotnet test.

This applies in particular to:
  - anything that queries through a value object
  - anything that binds an enumeration from a request body
  - anything a migration or a seeder touches
  - any date rendered to a member, which must be checked in a zone other than the server's

Verify the frontend with npm run build, never with tsc --noEmit. The build script is tsc -b && vite
build, and project-reference mode applies stricter settings than a bare --noEmit.

A container reporting healthy is not evidence that it runs your code: docker compose up -d --build
keeps the previous image when the build fails, and the stale container stays healthy. After a
rebuild, confirm the image was replaced.

When a defect is found this way, add the regression test that would have caught it, then fix it.

## RULE 23 — PERSISTING VALUE OBJECTS
Map a value object as an owned type, not with a value converter, whenever any of its members is ever
read in a query.

A converter collapses the object into an opaque scalar, so the provider cannot see inside it:
book.Isbn.Value compiles, passes every unit test, and throws at run time. An owned type produces the
same single column and keeps its members queryable.

For a value object that is a struct, such as Money, use ComplexProperty — the same idea for value
types. A converter is acceptable only for a value object that is never read, filtered, ordered or
aggregated inside a query.

Stage 2 hit this three times: Isbn broke catalogue search, StarRating broke the rating average, and
Money broke ordering by price. All three compiled and passed every unit test.

## RULE 24 — AUDIT IS ITS OWN BOUNDED CONTEXT
AuditEntry and IAuditUnitOfWork live under Domain/Features/Audit/, not under identity. Four domains
append to the trail and none owns it.

Never inject IIdentityUnitOfWork just to write an audit row.

Write the entry inside the command handler, in the same transaction as the change it describes.
Never in a domain event handler: a reaction runs after the commit and may be lost, and a trail that
can silently skip a transition is not a trail.

## RULE 14 — LANGUAGE
All repository content is written in English: code, comments, specs, docs, commit messages, and UI copy.
